## The customer who noticed first

A mid-market support-automation team I worked with had a triage bot in production for six months. Generation quality drifted slowly — answers got hedgier, citations got vaguer, the bot started apologizing more. No alert ever fired. The on-call engineer found out about the degradation from a screenshot a customer pasted into a Slack channel that happened to be shared.

The grim part: the team had argued about exactly this failure mode in week one. Someone had said, on a whiteboard, "the model will get worse and we won't see it because our eval set is the same prompts we trained the prompts on." That sentence got photographed, lost, and never turned into a metric, a runbook, or an alert. Six months later it cost them an enterprise renewal.

The failure mode was nameable on day one. It was named on day one-hundred-eighty, by a customer, in a screenshot. That is the entire lesson.

## The high-school version

Before you launch a thing, you have to be able to finish this sentence: *"This system will fail when ___, and we will know because ___."* If you can't finish it, you don't ship. If you can finish the first half but not the second half, you don't ship either — you've named a ghost, not a failure mode.

A failure mode isn't "it might break." A failure mode is a specific story about what breaks, in what conditions, with what symptom, observable through what signal. Until that story exists in writing, your customers are your monitoring system.

## The college version

A *named failure mode* is a four-tuple:

- **Trigger** — the condition under which the failure occurs (input drift, traffic spike, upstream API regression, model swap, prompt change).
- **Symptom** — what the system actually does wrong (hallucinated citation, latency cliff, refusal loop, silent truncation).
- **Signal** — the measurable thing that moves before or during the symptom (eval delta, p99 latency, refusal rate, embedding distance, customer ticket category).
- **Owner** — the human or rotation responsible for responding when the signal moves.

A system "ships with the failure mode named" when, for every plausible failure class, all four slots are filled and written down somewhere a tired on-call can find at 2am. Anything less is wishful thinking dressed as engineering. The artifact matters: a wiki page, a runbook entry, a row in a spreadsheet — pick a format, but make it discoverable and dated.

## The PhD version

This sits in the literature on *operational readiness reviews* and *failure mode and effects analysis* (FMEA), tools that predate software by decades — they come out of aerospace and reliability engineering in the 1940s-60s. Google's SRE practice formalized a software flavor of this with the production readiness review (see Beyer et al., *Site Reliability Engineering*, O'Reilly 2016, ch. 27 and 32). The novel wrinkle for LLM systems is that the symptom-to-signal mapping is no longer monotonic: a model can degrade along axes (sycophancy, citation faithfulness, instruction adherence) that don't show up in classical SLO instrumentation. So FMEA for ML systems requires *behavioral* SLOs, not just operational ones — closer in spirit to ML-Ops research like Sculley et al., "Hidden Technical Debt in Machine Learning Systems" (NeurIPS 2015).

## The trade-offs

Naming failure modes isn't free. It costs calendar time before launch, it costs political capital because someone has to say "we are not ready," and it costs ongoing instrumentation budget because every named mode wants its own signal and dashboard.

The determinism lever you're pulling is **process**, which is the lowest-leverage rung on the ladder per token of effort, but the highest-leverage rung per dollar before any code exists. Process can't fix a fundamentally non-deterministic component, but it can prevent you from learning about non-determinism through customer escalations. The trade is: you absorb a 5-15% pre-launch slowdown in exchange for a roughly order-of-magnitude reduction in mean-time-to-detect once live. If you're a team that gets graded on launch dates and not on incident postmortems, you will under-invest here. That's the failure mode of the failure-mode practice itself, and worth naming too.

## The war story

Back to the support-automation team. The failure was *eval-set staleness*: their offline evaluation prompts had been written by the same engineer who wrote the system prompt, six months prior, and were essentially a regression test of "does the prompt still work on the prompts the prompt was tuned on." Real customer traffic had drifted — more multilingual queries, more multi-turn clarifications, more edge cases involving refunds. The model handled the eval set fine and the production traffic worse and worse.

Naming this in week one would have cost an afternoon. The fix was a rotating sample of real production conversations, scrubbed and re-graded weekly, with a simple drift metric on grader scores. Implementing that fix in week twenty-six cost three engineers two sprints, an apology to the customer, and the renewal. The artifact that would have saved them was one paragraph in a runbook: *"The model will silently degrade as customer traffic distribution drifts from our eval set. We will know because weekly grader scores on rotating production samples will trend down. Owner: ML platform on-call."* One paragraph.

## Prototype assignment

Take any system you've shipped or are about to ship. Open a blank document. Write down five plausible failure modes, each as the four-tuple: trigger, symptom, signal, owner. Now — and this is the part that matters — go check whether the signal actually exists as instrumentation today. For every signal that doesn't exist, you've found pre-production work that you were about to externalize onto your customers. Do this in an afternoon. Do it before every launch, forever.

## Spirit

The determinism ladder is usually framed as architectural — Model, API, LoRA, RAG, Skills, MCP, Agents — but the ladder rests on a process foundation, and the foundation is *naming*. You cannot push work down the stack toward determinism if you can't articulate what non-determinism would look like when it bites. Every lever above this one assumes you already know what you're trying to constrain. Ship with the failure mode named, or accept that your customers are your observability stack — and they bill at a much higher rate than Datadog.